<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:windows">
<!--
    kuna issue testcase: the frame cookie MSVC stores on the stack is no
    argument of a call made below it.

    Issue:      kuna GH-1593 - the /GS cookie pushed in an SEH prologue
                (`mov eax,[___security_cookie]; xor eax,esp; push eax`) is
                printed as a stack argument of calls in the body.
    Stage:      P4 calls - checkInputTrialUse's stack trials
                (`p4_calls/kuna_stackcookie.rs`, `funcdata_callsite.rs`).

    stackcookie_pe_i386.exe (clean-room, source and recipe in
    stackcookie_pe_i386.s): gsseh (0x401000) pushes the cookie below an SEH
    frame and calls lookup("NAME") through a pointer, cleaning up after it;
    gsthis (0x401050) calls a method on this+8 with no stack argument under
    the same frame; gsebp (0x401090) keeps the cookie at [ebp-4] and checks it
    before returning. No call takes the cookie (#1-#3), and the check still
    gets it in ECX (#4). Three real arguments look like the cookie and stay:
    keyarg (0x401130) pushes key ^ esp with no SEH frame (#5); keyread
    (0x401150) pushes it under an SEH frame to a callee that reads it, and
    loses only the cookie above it (#6, #7); copyarg (0x4011a0) pushes a copy
    read back from the cookie's slot, which is an argument, while the call
    before it loses the cookie (#8, #9). Two more push such a value in the
    body of an SEH function, through a pointer: sehkey (0x4011f0) passes
    key ^ esp and 7 under a frame with a cookie, and keeps both while the
    cookie leaves (#10); sehebp (0x401240) passes key ^ ebp under an EBP
    frame with no cookie (#11). Only a value the prologue stores before it
    links the frame into fs:[0] is the cookie.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
